A||N||14||GT GT 87||Plastic impeller V-6 water pump||||1||
Z||000000||GT GT 87||09-26-2001||08:03 PM||||  I am building a new motor (3.2), and want everything perfect. I got a new V-6 water pump from The Fiero Store today , and it has a plastic impeller. All the pumps that I have changed in the past , had metal impellers . Does anyone has any experence with this type ? The rebuilt ones I have used were metal!!!  THANKS||||reg||1
Z||000001||sqoach||09-26-2001||08:13 PM||||I've heard to stay away from the plastic impellar pumps. I'd swap it out if I were you.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">           <BR>^^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^^<BR><LI><FONT SIZE="1">84 SE (FOR SALE)*SOLD(?)*<BR><LI>85 Coupe (parts car)($50)*sale pending*<BR><LI>86 SC</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||09-26-2001||08:22 PM||||I know how you feel.  I bought a "new" not rebuilt water pump from Napa and the impeller was plastic.  So I returned it and went to Pep boys and then Auto zone.  Every store carried the plastic impeller.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  <P><p>[This message has been edited by Leadfoot (edited 09-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||c m west||09-26-2001||08:51 PM||||I'm on the same search too for a metal waterpump, I'll let you know what brand it is once I find one||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shaun41178||09-26-2001||09:27 PM||||Hey Stever!  Hope the buildup is coming along good.  Asking about teh waterpump probably means the shortblock is togethor?  <P>Yes stay away from teh plastic impellars.  they don't flow as well as a metal one.  I have never had a problem with getting plastic impellars and I have changed a few fiero waterpumps in my day.  I got mine from Murrays Discount auto parts.  I think more of a regional thing near michigan.  Don't know how far they go though.<P>I would just take it back and ask the guy to check for a pump with a metal impellar.  Or get a rebuilt one.  I would get rebuilt ones and they were always metal.  You could try that.  Hope this helps.<P>Email about the progress of teh buildup.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87GTZ34||09-26-2001||09:38 PM||||Shaun, how many hp was your cooling system targeted at?  I have used the plastic impellers for my Fieros and other cars.  Currently, I have a stock '87 GT w/ 156 k miles, corrected any and all problems with my cooling system and guess what???  I now have an underheating problem.  Yes that's right, I live in south Florida where the summer temps are in the high 80s to mid 90s with 95% humidity, and it takes my car forever to get to temp and stay there.  Believe it or not, the Fiero engineers allowed some margin in the cooling system for the power output of the 2.8.   Bottom line is unless your power plant is putting out 250 or more horses, the stock cooling system running a plastic impeller works like a champ provided the system is in good working order.  Of course this is just my experience, your mileage may vary.  Also for those that are sticklers for reducing parasitic power loss, plastic is lighter than metal.  Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000006||foxgapfiero||09-26-2001||09:57 PM||||Stay away from those plastic impellers. A good friend had one on his and the thing would slip on the shaft after the car reached temp. I finally settled on a lifetime replacement rebuid for my 3.4 after I couldn't get a new one from NAPA, Car Quest, Auto zone or any of the other stores in the area. All of them had the same plastic impeller. The rebuild had a cast iron. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||09-26-2001||10:21 PM||||I don't know if 3.4 and 2.8 w/p's are different, but I've replaced two 2.8 water-pumps in my Fieros, and AutoZone has rebuilt GM stamped water-pumps with correct impeller for $12.99 in stock. Never any trouble at all. Aluminum body, cast impeller, gasket and instructions.||||reg||1
Z||000008||8Ball||09-26-2001||11:29 PM||||I have bought two rebuilt water pumps in the last month. I broke the first one tightening it. Both of them had metal impellors. One was from Auto Zone and the other Advanced Auto. Guess I got lucky.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||09-26-2001||11:43 PM||||From what I hear, since the Fiero WP has to push the coolant all the way forward to the radiator and back (much farther than in a front engine car) the plastic impellers can't take the load.  They work fine in FWD cars where the engine is close to the radiator, but fail quickly when used in a Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dslice||09-26-2001||11:56 PM||||The dealership put a plastic one i my car. the impeller seperated from the driveshaft and wouldnt move enough coolant, Hence it overheated and I blew a head gasket. I made sure the next pump was metal. The plastic one failed after less then two years. The big problem is that when the plastic one fails you may not notice it at first cause at higher rpms it still moves enough coolant, but at idle look out! In most cases you can tell if you got an aftermarket pump if the pump has an extra hose outlet that isnt used. The factory one doesnt have that. I wouldnt blame the fiero store, the dealer gave me a plastic one, but I would return it and see if they carry the metal impeller(which might be more$).<P><P>------------------<BR>Rick<P>86GT<BR>84COUP<BR>http://home.mn.rr.com/gmfierogt||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||09-27-2001||01:44 AM||||I have a spare brand new water pump in my garage. Metal impeller. But I don't have gaskets. It's just sitting on the shelf.<BR>I have heard too many bad things about the plastic ones.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B>||||reg||12
Z||000012||artherd||09-27-2001||03:00 AM||||I got a new one from the GM dealer, metal impeller.<P>The plastic impellers WILL seperate from the shaft, and stop working... this is bad... and an infamous Fiero problem.  <P>I've got an extra GM one too, see The Mall.<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000013||88 Database Nut||09-27-2001||10:00 AM||||GT GT 87,<BR>Definately avoid the H2O pumps with the plastic impellers.  I put one on my 88GT one time, because I didn't check it at the NAPA store and didn't feel like driving the 1 hour round trip to get another.  It worked fine for about 18 months, then one day, out of the blue, my car started running warmer than normal.  Since my commute was usually stop-free, I didn't investigate it when it first happened.  It only took a couple of minutes at a traffic light to convince me something was very wrong!  Turns out the plastic impeller disk cracked across the shaft and was not turning once the coolant heated up a little.  Napa's replacement also had a plastic impeller, but I rejected it on the spot and they brought out a "Gold" series water pump, which had a very aggressive looking metal impeller (much better than the factory pump's, and very much like the one for my wife's Honda).  The plastic one was a "Carter" wp, which was the same as the one they dealer tried to sell me!  Don't know where you lucky guys found so many metal impeller ones, I had to look all over heck here in Atlanta to finally find one.<P>I guess we're all in agreement on this!||||reg||1
Z||000014||Back On Holiday||09-28-2001||10:54 PM||||88 Database nut is correct, napa has a Gold Series which has the metal one.  thats what I installed on mine.||||reg||1
